int vertex sets, but it never explicitly requires the root v to be disjoint from the teeth and all blocks. The closing assertion that comb disjointness puts v outside every block does not establish that.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"def-chain-homotopy","concern":"The definition uses f_n-g_n and d s+s d without stating an additive or abelian ambient category. Its cited graded-morphism definition is itself stated for complexes in an abelian category.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"def-null-homotopic-chain-map","concern":"The zero chain map, addition in the displayed formula, and the Hom complex are used without an additive or abelian ambient-category hypothesis.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"prop-chain-homotopy-is-an-equivalence-relation","concern":"The proof uses the zero family, negatives, and sums of homotopies without an additive or abelian ambient-category hypothesis.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"lem-chain-homotopy-is-compatible-with-addition-and-composition","concern":"The statement and proof use sums and additivity of Ch(A), but do not state the additive-category hypothesis required by the cited result.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"prop-null-homotopic-maps-form-a-two-sided-additive-ideal","concern":"The claimed additive ideal, zero maps, and sums require an additive ambient category, but no such hypothesis appears in the statement.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"lem-a-bipartite-layer-is-small-unless-a-large-comb-already-appears","concern":"[L1] incompletely restates the cited comb definition: it omits the blockade/width requirement and tooth-block disjointness. Step 3.1 separately supplies the relevant size and bipartition facts, so this appears to be a citation-presentation gap rather than a counterexample to the lemma.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"}],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[]}